drm/apple: show ProMotion as adaptive sync on internal panels, opt-in

With appledrm.experimental_vrr=1 the connector of an internal ProMotion
panel gets the standard "vrr_capable" property, set while the panel
offers its 120 Hz timing, and the CRTC's VRR_ENABLED chooses how the DCP
firmware runs the panel in that timing. No modeset is involved.

Read from the DCP 13.5 firmware (t602x image of the Asahi stub, the
firmware these machines boot Linux with):

 - In the 120 Hz timing (TimingElements ID 2, the mode parse_mode()
   marks vrr) UPPipeDCP_H13P::init_m3_timing_parameters() bounds the
   panel's frame duration to 2..10 subframes of 100000 ticks of the
   24 MHz clock (4.167 ms each), so 120..24 Hz. macOS publishes the same
   bounds in IOMFBDisplayRefresh (min/max interval 200000/1000000 mach
   ticks, step 100000). The "promoted" 60/59.94/50/48/47.95 Hz timings
   get a fixed 4 or 5 subframes.
 - swap_flush_stream() hands every swap to the panel's timing controller
   with the swap record's first timestamp (ts1) and those bounds. Only a
   swap whose ts1 is 0 is forced to 4 subframes (60 Hz), which is why the
   120 Hz timing needs the non-zero timestamps iomfb_flush() sets there.
   So with those timestamps the panel should already refresh when a swap
   arrives, between 120 and 24 Hz, and repeat the last frame otherwise:
   adaptive sync. VRR_ENABLED=1 leaves it at that.
 - VRR_ENABLED=0 holds the panel at the timing's nominal 120 Hz with the
   firmware's APTFixedRR runtime property (ID 74 in its property name
   table) set to 2 subframes, through UnifiedPipeline2::applyProperty
   (A352, id and value as two u32s). apt_fixed_rr_changed() accepts a
   value within the timing's bounds and logs "forcing refresh rate: %d
   subframes" in DCP's syslog; 0 restores the default ("forcing refresh
   rate: disabled").

The property change is sent ahead of the next swap, so a commit that only
toggles VRR_ENABLED takes effect with the next frame. After a modeset that
followed a fixed rate it is sent again, which also clears a rate the new
timing cannot do. With VRR_ENABLED set from the start no applyProperty is
ever sent.

IOMFBParameter_adaptive_sync (minRR) is left alone: in the 13.5 firmware
the stored minRR is only read on the external DP/HDMI path (Adaptive-Sync
SDP, vtotal extension), never when the internal panel's timing is set up.
External Adaptive-Sync displays are AsahiLinux/linux#477's subject and
are out of scope here.

Only 13.5 firmware is covered, since the method tag, the property ID and
the timing controller logic were read from it; vrr_capable stays 0 on
other firmware and on external connectors. Untested on hardware.

Without the parameter no property is attached, the APTFixedRR state stays
0, and every swap goes out exactly as before.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/apple_drv.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/apple_drv.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/apple_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/apple_drv.c
@@ -357,6 +357,10 @@ static int apple_probe_per_dcp(struct device *dev,
 	crtc->dcp = dcp;
 	dcp_link(dcp, crtc, connector);
 
+	ret = dcp_attach_vrr_property(&connector->base);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	return drm_connector_attach_encoder(&connector->base, &enc->base);
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp-internal.h b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp-internal.h
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp-internal.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp-internal.h
@@ -209,6 +209,15 @@ struct apple_dcp {
 	/* enable CRC calculation */
 	bool crc_enabled;
 
+	/*
+	 * appledrm.experimental_vrr: the APTFixedRR value last sent to DCP and
+	 * the one the current CRTC state wants (0: refresh follows the swaps,
+	 * the firmware's default; N: fixed at N 240 Hz subframes). U32_MAX
+	 * after a modeset that followed a fixed rate, until the next swap.
+	 */
+	u32 apt_fixed_rr;
+	u32 apt_fixed_rr_want;
+
 	/* Modes valid for the connected display */
 	struct dcp_display_mode *modes;
 	unsigned int nr_modes;
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/apple/dcp.c
@@ -54,6 +54,11 @@ bool hdmi_audio;
 module_param(hdmi_audio, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(hdmi_audio, "Enable unstable HDMI audio support");
 
+bool dcp_experimental_vrr;
+module_param_named(experimental_vrr, dcp_experimental_vrr, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(experimental_vrr, "Expose ProMotion on internal panels as "
+		 "vrr_capable and follow VRR_ENABLED (DCP 13.5 firmware, untested)");
+
 static bool unstable_edid = true;
 module_param(unstable_edid, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(unstable_edid, "Enable unstable EDID retrival support");
@@ -523,6 +528,41 @@ void dcp_link(struct platform_device *pdev, struct apple_crtc *crtc,
 	dcp->connector = connector;
 }
 
+/*
+ * With appledrm.experimental_vrr, attach "vrr_capable" before the device is
+ * registered; dcp_update_vrr_capable() sets it once the modes are known.
+ */
+int dcp_attach_vrr_property(struct drm_connector *connector)
+{
+	if (!dcp_experimental_vrr)
+		return 0;
+
+	return drm_connector_attach_vrr_capable_property(connector);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Only the internal ProMotion panels in their 120 Hz timing (the modes
+ * parse_mode() marks vrr) vary their refresh, and only the 13.5 firmware's
+ * handling of that has been looked at. See dcp_swap_vrr().
+ */
+void dcp_update_vrr_capable(struct apple_dcp *dcp)
+{
+	bool capable = false;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	if (!dcp_experimental_vrr || !dcp->connector)
+		return;
+
+	if (dcp->connector->connected &&
+	    dcp->fw_compat == DCP_FIRMWARE_V_13_5 &&
+	    dcp->connector_type == DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_eDP) {
+		for (i = 0; i < dcp->nr_modes; i++)
+			capable |= dcp->modes[i].vrr;
+	}
+
+	drm_connector_set_vrr_capable_property(&dcp->connector->base, capable);
+}
+
 
 bool dcp_fw_compat_is_12_x(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
